{
  "abstract": "A machine-checked proof of Gisin's theorem for two finite-dimensional systems of any dimensions: a pure state violates the CHSH inequality, for some choice of +/-1-valued observables on each side, if and only if it is not a product state. A vector of the joint system is a function psi : m x n -> C on a product basis, an observable is a Hermitian involution, the correlation of A and B is <psi, (A (x) B) psi> with Mathlib's Kronecker product, and the CHSH expression is <A0 B0> + <A0 B1> + <A1 B0> - <A1 B1>. Also proved separately: a unit product vector satisfies |CHSH| <= 2 for all such observables. The proof of the violation splits off two Schmidt terms of a non-product vector without a full singular value decomposition, from two eigenvectors of Psi Psi^H with positive eigenvalues, measures Z and X on Alice's two-dimensional span and cZ +/- tX on Bob's, with c and t chosen from the two Schmidt coefficients sigma0, sigma1, and acts as the identity on the orthogonal complements; the rest of the vector then contributes exactly its squared norm times 2, and the total is 2 + 2(sqrt(N^2 + C^2) - N) > 2, where N = sigma0^2 + sigma1^2 and C = 2 sigma0 sigma1 > 0. Controls exercise a product vector, the Bell state, a weakly entangled two-qubit state and a 3 x 3 state with three Schmidt terms.",
